{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Let Reuben live</b>. Ibn Ezra (on Deuteronomy 33:6) said that he began with Reuben because he is the firstborn, and he did not mention Simeon on account of the sin of Peor, for the Simeonites were the ones who served, and their number bears witness, and their prince was also killed. And so is the opinion of Rashi and the commentators. But in my opinion their number is no proof, because they still lacked thirteen thousand and more besides those who died in the plague, and also the rest of the tribes lacked somewhat, for the children of Gad lacked five thousand, and the children of Ephraim eight thousand. And furthermore, the verse that says (Numbers 25:3), \"And Israel joined himself to Baal Peor,\" and it says (ibid. v. 4), \"Take all the heads of the people\" — it appears that there were among them men from all the tribes, and all their judges would judge them, and so it says (ibid. v. 11), \"and I did not consume the children of Israel in my jealousy.\" And do not answer me from the matter of Achan, of whom it says (Joshua 7:11), \"Israel has sinned,\" for there the sin was not made known and he mentions them generically. And likewise the slain man is no proof, for he was a prince of a father's house among them, not the prince of the tribe. And those who gathered against YHWH in the congregation of Korah were greater and more honored than he, for they were princes of the congregation, yet not all their tribes followed after them. And the Psalm mentioned Dathan and Abiram and their matter by name (Psalms 106:17), \"The earth opened and swallowed Dathan, and covered the company of Abiram,\" and it mentioned (ibid. v. 28), \"They joined themselves to Baal Peor,\" together with (ibid. v. 24), \"They despised the desirable land,\" to include the entire people. And far be it that Moses would withhold blessing Simeon and not wipe out a tribe from Israel, since all those who joined themselves to Baal Peor had already perished, as it says (Deuteronomy 4:3), \"for every man who went after Baal Peor, YHWH your Elohim destroyed him from your midst,\" and these, the remaining ones, are all written of (ibid. v. 4), \"But you who cleave to YHWH your Elohim are alive, all of you, this day\" — and why should he not bless them? Behold, they all made the Golden Calf and sinned with the spies, and it was atoned for them and he blessed them. And I saw in Midrash Rabbah (Numbers Rabbah 21:9) that six families were lost on account of the harlotry at the counsel of Balaam, and it counts one family for Simeon and five for the children of Benjamin. Now according to their opinion, Benjamin sinned more than Simeon in that sin, yet he is included in the blessing. And the correct view in my eyes is that Scripture never counts the tribes of Israel except twelve, and so it says in the blessing of Jacob (Genesis 49:28), \"All these are the tribes of Israel, twelve.\" Now Jacob mentioned his twelve sons and counted Joseph as one tribe, but Moses saw fit to count two tribes for Joseph, as he said (Deuteronomy 33:17), \"and they are the tens of thousands of Ephraim and they are the thousands of Manasseh.\" And this was for two reasons: first, because after the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded and made them two tribes in the dedication of the altar, in the banners, and in the inheritance of the land, it was necessary to count them as two in their blessing. And furthermore, because he mentioned Joshua, who would assign them their inheritance of the land, and he was from Ephraim the younger, it was necessary to mention his older brother. Now he wished to bless Levi, for through his blessing all Israel would be blessed, that their offerings would find favor before HaShem. And it was necessary to leave out one of the tribes, since they are never counted anywhere except twelve, corresponding to the twelve signs of the zodiac in the firmament, and the twelve months in the year, and the twelve diagonal boundaries, which they said in the Midrash are the arms of the world, and as the Sages mentioned in tractate Berakhot (32a): the Holy One, blessed be He, said to the prophet, \"Go and say to the Congregation of Israel: My daughter, twelve signs I created in the firmament corresponding to the twelve tribes.\" And likewise at Mount Gerizim and Mount Ebal (Deuteronomy 27:12), where Levi is counted, the name Joseph is not divided into two tribes. And so you will see in the division of Ezekiel (48:4–5), that in the division of the land he counts Joseph as two tribes and does not mention Levi, and when he counts the gates of the city he mentions (ibid. v. 31) \"the gate of Levi, one\" and does not count the tribe of Joseph as two tribes, but rather says (ibid. v. 32), \"the gate of Joseph, one\" — for the tribes of Israel shall never be counted except twelve. And I have already mentioned this in the portion of Korah (Numbers 17:17). Now he left out Simeon, whose tribe was not great and in whom the blessing of Jacob their father was not abundant — rather, \"I will divide them in Jacob and scatter them in Israel\" (Genesis 49:7) — and behold, they too will be blessed through the blessing of the rest of the tribes among whom they dwell. And the order of this blessing was through the Holy Spirit according to the manner of their inheritance: he began with Reuben because he took his inheritance first, and also because he is the firstborn, and Moses prayed for him that his name not be blotted out on account of his sin, and therefore he granted him in his blessing the right of the firstborn. And afterward he gave precedence to Judah, for he is the first to inherit in the land, and the ruler comes from him, and he is the one who goes up first to battle, in which he would bless him — and behold, his blessing encompasses all Israel. And afterward he blessed Levi, who would camp with the children of Judah in Jerusalem, and there their offerings would find favor. And after that he blessed Benjamin, whose inheritance was with the children of Judah, and the city of Jerusalem and the Temple between the children of Judah and the children of Benjamin, and the Levites would camp with both. And after that he blessed the children of Joseph, for so was their inheritance, as it says (Joshua 18:11), \"And the lot of the tribe of the children of Benjamin came up for their families, and the territory of their lot went out between the children of Judah and the children of Joseph.\" And after that he finished blessing the children of the matriarchs, and he gave precedence to Zebulun over Issachar, as Jacob did (Genesis 49:13–14), and so their lot fell: the third to the children of Zebulun (Joshua 19:10), and the fourth lot to the children of Issachar (ibid. v. 17). And after that he blessed the children of the maidservants — Dan, Naphtali, and Asher — in the order of their genealogies, which is their banner. And he gave precedence to Gad over all the children of the maidservants, for he took his inheritance first, together with Reuben who precedes, and he was of the banner of Reuben.\n\nThe meaning of \"Let Reuben live\" is that his tribe shall live and never die. And \"let his men be a number (מִסְפָּר)\" — as in \"and I have not learned wisdom, and the knowledge of the holy ones I shall know (אֵדָע)\" (Proverbs 30:3) — means: let his men not be a number, that they not be few, for anything that is counted is little; and so too, \"and I am men of number (מְתֵי מִסְפָּר)\" (Genesis 34:30) — this is the opinion of Ibn Ezra (on Deuteronomy 33:6). But it is more correct to interpret \"Let Reuben live and not die\" as meaning: let his tribe not be cut off at any time, and let his countings be in the number of the children of Israel forever — he prays for him that his sin and the great anger with which his father was angry at him for defiling his bed not cause the blotting out of his name from Israel. And this is like the matter said there (Genesis 35:22), \"And Reuben went and lay with Bilhah his father's concubine, and Israel heard, and the sons of Jacob were twelve\" — for it informs that because of his sin he did not depart from the count; and so Rashi explained (on Deuteronomy 33:6). And the meaning of <b>his men (מְתָיו)</b> is that all his men shall merit this, and not even part of the tribe shall be cut off for his sin. And perhaps <b>מְתָיו</b> alludes to the heads of his four families, and the meaning of <b>מִסְפָּר</b> would be like בְּמִסְפָּר (\"in number\"), and so too לִזְנוֹת בֵּית אָבִיהָ (Deuteronomy 22:21), יִרְחֲצוּ מַיִם (Exodus 30:20), and many like these. Or its meaning is that his men should be the number of Israel — that he should be counted first according to the right of the firstborn, for the one is the number (מִסְפָּר) since from him comes all.\n\nAnd Onkelos (on Deuteronomy 33:6) translated: \"and his sons shall receive their inheritance in their count.\" He intends what we have explained: he says that Reuben shall live and not be cut off, and his men shall be in their number forever — his tribe shall not be wiped out from Israel, nor shall they be counted beyond their number to become two tribes; he stripped the birthright from him and promised him one tribe forever.\n\nAnd there are commentators who say that this blessing concerns the conquest of the land: he says that Reuben shall live as they cross over armed for war before the children of Israel, and the meaning is that they shall prevail and not be defeated, like the meaning of \"and by your sword you shall live\" (Genesis 27:40). \"And let him not die\" — that none of them shall fall in battle. \"And let his men be a number\" — that they shall return to their tents in their number, and not a single man shall be missing. And so in the blessing of Gad his companion, he mentioned this matter. And this interpretation is close, but the first is more correct in my eyes, for the blessing of Moses follows the pattern that Jacob mentioned and the matter thereof.\n\nAnd by way of truth, \"Let Reuben live and not die\" is because of the reason of \"visiting the iniquity of the fathers upon the children\" (Exodus 20:5), and there (ibid.) I alluded to its secret. And the blessing of Moses served to atone for him the matter of Bilhah, so that he would not be cut off and die on its account forever; but He visited upon him together with the rest of the tribes the matter of Joseph, according to what is found in the chapters of the Heikhalot."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "The phrase ועיר ירושלים appears in one witness (marked ס\"א = שני אוה\"ג, a variant reading); the other witnesses read simply ועיר ירושלים without that mark. I rendered the content without noting the variant in the body, as the sense is identical."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:00:49.343606916Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}